[observation.3.7.5]
task = "3.7"
file = "platform/hosted/dashboard/web/next.config.ts"
symbol = "turbopack.root platform-hosted-smoke"
observed = "Running the 3.7 verify_cmd on main 1081a776 after observation 3.7.4: six images built and the layerx-dashboard-web image passed test:static but failed at next build with TurbopackInternalError Symlink [project]/node_modules is invalid, it points out of the filesystem root, because the dashboard resolves its node_modules through the symlink to human/apps/web that developer-dashboard-install and the Dockerfile both create and Turbopack treated the dashboard directory as its root. Reproduced on the host with the same symlink. The gate was also run with MAKEFLAGS=-j3, which let make start platform-hosted-smoke concurrently with platform-beta-cluster-up; the verify_cmd is only meaningful serially. Repairs: next.config.ts sets turbopack.root and outputFileTracingRoot to the repository root (next build then succeeds on the host, standalone output at .next/standalone/platform/hosted/dashboard/web/server.js) and the Dockerfile runtime stage copies .next/static and starts server.js from that mirrored path; platform-hosted-smoke sources the env file beta-cluster.sh writes (PLATFORM_BETA_CLUSTER_ENV, default build/beta-cluster/env) when present before checking its inputs, so the chained verify_cmd can consume the exported endpoints in the same make invocation, and it now names the first missing input instead of failing silently (verified: make platform-hosted-smoke without inputs prints missing input LAYERX_TESTNET_URL and exits 1). The smoke still requires LAYERX_TEST_SOURCE_PUBLIC_KEY, LAYERX_TEST_DESTINATION_DID, LAYERX_TEST_ASSET and LAYERX_TEST_AMOUNT, which neither the cluster script nor the task text supplies."
assumption = "Both repairs are task-scoped wiring of the 3.7 bring-up and its chained gate, not weakenings; the four smoke-only inputs are owner inputs the smoke stage will report blocked by name unless supplied in the environment."
severity = "note"

[observation.3.7.6]
task = "3.7"
file = "platform/hosted/registry/node-provision-build-boundary.sh"
symbol = "chmod 0700 losetup -d -O AUTOCLEAR"
observed = "Running the 3.7 verify_cmd serially on main bd0cc98b: platform-test-tooling passed, all seven images built and loaded into the kind nodes, and cluster-up failed installing the registry node boundary: layerx-program-registry-boundary.service exited 1 with chmod: changing permissions of /sys/fs/cgroup/layerx-program-registry: Operation not permitted. The unit from commit 7bf942eb bounds the service to CAP_SYS_ADMIN CAP_CHOWN CAP_DAC_OVERRIDE (pinned by delegation_quota_and_open_inode_execution_fail_closed) while the provisioner chowned the cgroup directory and the slot mountpoints to 4030:4030 before chmod 0700, which needs CAP_FOWNER once root no longer owns the inode. Replaying the script in a privileged container with cap_fowner dropped reproduced the failure at the same chmod. The same replay then showed losetup --find --show --autoclear is rejected as unrecognized option by util-linux 2.38.1 (the kind node's Debian bookworm) and 2.39.3 (host), so the provisioner could never have mounted a build slot. Repairs: chmod now precedes chown at both sites and is skipped when the mode is already 0700 so a unit restart over a provisioned node passes; the loop device is attached with losetup --find --show, mounted, then detached with losetup -d so the kernel sets LO_FLAGS_AUTOCLEAR on the busy device, and the script asserts losetup -n -O AUTOCLEAR reports 1. The two contract tests that pinned the literal --autoclear now pin -O AUTOCLEAR, the assertion the script actually makes. Replay with cap_fowner dropped: first run and idempotent second run exit 0, cgroup and both slot mountpoints are 4030:4030 mode 700, subtree_control delegates cpu io memory pids, both loops report AUTOCLEAR 1 and are released after umount. cargo test for platform/hosted/registry passes (14 tests)."
assumption = "Both defects are pre-existing in the task 3.x registry boundary and surfaced only because 3.7 is the first gate to run the unit under systemd on a real node; the capability set of the unit is unchanged and the test literal change replaces a flag that does not exist with the check the script performs."
severity = "note"

[observation.3.7.7]
task = "3.7"
file = "platform/hosted/registry/node-provision-build-boundary.sh"
symbol = "mkfs.ext4 -N inodes_per_group"
observed = "Rerunning the 3.7 verify_cmd on main df5561c0 after observation 3.7.6: all images rebuilt and loaded, the boundary unit passed the cgroup delegation, then exited 1 at test inode_count -le INODES because mkfs.ext4 -N 65536 on the 5 GiB slot image produced 65920 inodes; mke2fs rounds the inode count up to a whole number of inodes per block group, so the provisioner's own quota assertion could not pass for the unit's shipped numbers. Reproduced by replaying the script on the kind worker with the unit's environment. Repair: the provisioner fixes the geometry with -b 4096 -g 32768 -I 256, derives the block-group count from the byte quota, and requests the largest multiple of 16 inodes per group that keeps the total at or under the inode quota (65280 for the shipped 5 GiB and 65536 values); it exits 64 if fewer than 16 inodes per group would remain. Replay in a privileged container with cap_fowner dropped using the unit's numbers: four slots provisioned, inode count 65280, block count 1310720 of 4096 bytes, autoclear set on all four loops, first and second runs exit 0, loops released after umount; the 64 MiB / 1024 inode case yields exactly 1024 inodes. cargo test for platform/hosted/registry passes."
assumption = "The byte and inode quotas remain upper bounds enforced by the existing assertions; a filesystem with slightly fewer inodes than the quota is the only way mke2fs can honour a ceiling."
severity = "note"

[observation.3.7.8]
task = "3.7"
file = "platform/hosted/tests/beta-cluster.sh"
symbol = "port_forward wait_ready"
observed = "Rerunning the 3.7 verify_cmd on main 01049d26 after observation 3.7.7: images built and loaded, the registry node boundary unit became active on the kind worker, secrets, CA and manifests applied, and bring-up failed at port-forward to layerx-developer/layerx-webhooks with kubectl's error unable to forward port because pod is not running. Current status=Pending. The script started its four port-forwards immediately after manifests_apply, and kubectl exits when the pod it selects for a Service is still Pending; the testnet, gateway and faucet forwards happened to select pods that had already started. Repair: port_forward now relaunches kubectl while it exits with pod is not running, for up to 90 launches about two seconds apart, and keeps the original failure paths for any other exit or a forward that never reports readiness. bash -n passes. With the cluster left up after the failure, every LayerX pod except dashboard-web stayed 0/1: layerx-program-registry-0 is unschedulable because persistentvolumeclaim layerx-program-builder-release is not found (the volume the builder_release_publish step provisions from LAYERX_BETA_BUILDER_ENVIRONMENT_DIR), and the testnet-control readiness probe fails at its 1 second timeout while the pod logs connection failed: Broken pipe; a direct GET of /readyz through the port-forward returned an empty reply after 50.3 seconds. Journey readiness declares Identity, Core, CoreAdmin and ReceiptAuthority dependencies that only the LAYERX_BETA_EXTERNAL_MANIFESTS Services can provide, so wait_ready cannot succeed in this environment; whether /readyz answers within the probe timeout once those Services exist was not measurable."
assumption = "The port-forward retry removes an ordering race in the bring-up and changes no readiness criterion; the readiness findings are recorded for the owner and are not treated as task 3.7 defects."
severity = "note"

[observation.3.7.9]
task = "3.7"
file = "platform/hosted/tests/beta-cluster.sh"
symbol = "wait_ready MISSING_INPUTS"
observed = "Full serial run of the 3.7 verify_cmd on main 56d8ef97 (make platform-test-tooling platform-beta-cluster-up platform-hosted-smoke platform-beta-cluster-down, MAKEFLAGS unset): platform-test-tooling passed with 13 green cargo test suites and no failures; platform-beta-cluster-up built and loaded all seven images at revision 56d8ef97b32c, created the kind cluster with calico, activated the registry node boundary unit on the worker, generated the internal CA, sequencer key and test auth token, applied the manifests, established all four port-forwards, and wrote build/beta-cluster/env; wait_ready then failed after 900 seconds. Final state: faucet (3/3), faucet-redis, gateway-redis and dashboard-web (2/2) ready; gateway (3), testnet-control (2, 5 restarts each), dashboard-api (2) and webhooks (3) running but not ready; layerx-program-registry-0 Pending because the layerx-program-builder-release volume is only provisioned from LAYERX_BETA_BUILDER_ENVIRONMENT_DIR; testnet /readyz unreachable through the port-forward; the two status-publisher job pods in Error. The script reported the owner inputs by name: LAYERX_BETA_BUILDER_ENVIRONMENT_DIR (hermetic builder root filesystem for the layerx-program-builder-release volume) and LAYERX_BETA_EXTERNAL_MANIFESTS (trusted-boundary Services absent from layerx-testnet: layerx-pending-core layerx-pending-core-admin paxeer-boundary layerx-identity layerx-receipt-authority layerx-agent-boundary), and the env file records that LAYERX_QUALIFICATION_NODE_URL, _AGENT_URL, _HUMAN_URL and _PAXEER_URL were not exported for the same reason. platform-hosted-smoke and platform-beta-cluster-down did not run in the chain; the cluster was torn down afterwards with make platform-beta-cluster-down (teardown complete). Every journey declares at least one of the Identity, Core, CoreAdmin, ReceiptAuthority or Registry dependencies, so readiness cannot be reached without both inputs. Task 3.7 stays in_progress; cg spec done was not forced."
assumption = "The verify_cmd is complete and correct as written and will pass only when the owner supplies LAYERX_BETA_BUILDER_ENVIRONMENT_DIR and LAYERX_BETA_EXTERNAL_MANIFESTS (plus LAYERX_TEST_SOURCE_PUBLIC_KEY, LAYERX_TEST_DESTINATION_DID, LAYERX_TEST_ASSET and LAYERX_TEST_AMOUNT for the smoke stage) in the environment of the gate run; no fake Services, volumes or readiness shortcuts were introduced."
severity = "blocked"
